#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198047 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/id #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" :question/math-academy-id "e-12457" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7b2d027a761ba93f679cfb517709811938da412b06e59212391741b5660a2b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to \n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x-2) & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 3x-6 & =5x \\\\ 3x-5x & =6 \\\\ -2x & =6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229141 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "533cdb5e-1d90-5aae-961b-4fe7daa30b0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6617" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,\\underbrace{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-6618" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166654 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "fba5b411-86fe-5d2f-85ee-87cfa1e96d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8465" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$, compute $P(X = 2)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$, so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4^x e^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4^{2}e^{-4}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1465\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166655 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "540361c1-5d4e-5b88-a295-f606f5518f38" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "62d1e798-9a57-5657-9a6d-8fb70bff0ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8466" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(5)$, compute $P(X < 3)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(5)$, so the probability distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{5^x e^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 3) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5^{0}e^{-5}}{0!} + \\frac{5^{1}e^{-5}}{1!} + \\frac{5^{2}e^{-5}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1247\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166656 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21835800-1a05-56c1-a5ee-b8c2e1df2273" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c89b3081-38d7-5430-b912-82f84ef532ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8467" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, compute $P(2 < X < 5)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, so the probability distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 < X < 5) &= P(X ∈ {3, 4}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 3) + P(X = 4) \\\\\n&= f(3) + f(4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{3}e^{-3}}{3!} + \\frac{3^{4}e^{-3}}{4!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.3921\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166657 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6219a2a-8c28-57c1-a4ad-0ede62529376" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "d1e693a2-073f-5f8c-98fd-3b89496521d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8468" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, compute $P(X > 2)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, so the probability distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2$. However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 2) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{0}e^{-3}}{0!} + \\frac{3^{1}e^{-3}}{1!} + \\frac{3^{2}e^{-3}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.4232\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.4232 \\\\\n&= 0.5768\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198240 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/id #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" :question/math-academy-id "e-8690" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = 3x^2 - y^2$ given that $3x^2 + 2y^2\\leq 16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=\\left(0,  0 \\right).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2  = 16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x &=6\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right).$\n\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0,$ which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0,$ which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left( 0,0 \\right), \\qquad  \\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left( 0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left( \\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2  \\right),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198241 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8691" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of cosines of the angle measures of a triangle, given that all three angles are acute." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76caab9b-0240-534e-af0e-2cdf2d4653da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670412} {:answer/id #uuid "781b91a3-3b19-5b60-985e-d16b045ce80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670413} {:answer/id #uuid "921d2a5e-8350-597a-ad03-6c5e7df2d745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670420} {:answer/id #uuid "b4eb3827-e3c7-53d9-a7d4-dbd47e1d70fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670425} {:answer/id #uuid "c42e5e30-8e73-5876-901b-fc95ac997a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76caab9b-0240-534e-af0e-2cdf2d4653da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670412} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ad2725-54e9-5e8e-9e65-216f8922f0e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670444}] :question/id #uuid "1592a32d-005d-5261-a0e4-09fc6b72ccf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106322" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2=\\frac{7}{x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{x+3}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition and the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{7} \\\\ 7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+3}{7}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =\\cancel{7}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+3}{\\cancel{7}}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =x+3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c09cb2e-792c-5b50-9ddb-61b4ad09d949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670392} {:answer/id #uuid "590c1066-c2b3-5f96-ae20-75e02640b2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670407} {:answer/id #uuid "8d01fb2b-91b3-5128-be1a-d928008aa632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670416} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5f206d-9010-556a-98c3-a48c71b9e1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670435} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f8fc4b-e975-5087-9d1f-ddd9534cddff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "590c1066-c2b3-5f96-ae20-75e02640b2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670407} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce4cf6fe-52ce-57e9-b242-0bd13032b5ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670452}] :question/id #uuid "7c6b4a66-c5e9-5822-b5d3-41ccbed0cc84" :question/math-academy-id "q-106324" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{2}=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{2}=4\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+5}{2}\\right) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+5}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & =8 \\\\ x+5 & =8 \\\\ x+5-5 & =8-5 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30bc81e6-8fff-54e2-ac15-49e3fb530116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503070} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503245} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0aa041-1831-5a03-87fe-4c02c07d7e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503266} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dc91f3-4ee3-57a4-8547-157373bab8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503304} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fd723a-f248-5a39-96ba-76e1a1a6935a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503245} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c930558-3f13-59ed-984d-aa81dfac2ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106354" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x+15}{3}\\right) & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x+12}{3}\\right) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df4c844-fa64-5ffb-bf2f-20d32f4045de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0501" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670398} {:answer/id #uuid "3f12f7fe-2842-59fd-9972-d7eaaa457c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0223" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670403} {:answer/id #uuid "92041c52-6d1a-5884-a530-db5bd12d1433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670419} {:answer/id #uuid "c2063ee0-43c6-5e6e-814c-3d83f702e518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670427} {:answer/id #uuid "d51f2e3b-5862-59df-a943-5e2f59625754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51f2e3b-5862-59df-a943-5e2f59625754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670432} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9c4cbf-7c12-5198-9194-ef060c21ad92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670448}] :question/id #uuid "e8cab3f8-2d4f-5187-b7bf-4e4ac59dbfe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130048" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Po}(7),$ compute $P(X=3),$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Po}(\\lambda ),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Po}(7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{3}{e}^{-7}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.0521.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fd9c41-d3be-5ccf-8e93-a9c09b12180a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1755" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670389} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf60cfb-234c-5681-a9cc-69fccc778e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670406} {:answer/id #uuid "98aee249-615e-5aef-9c42-bac22058a161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1022" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670422} {:answer/id #uuid "cac8a4fd-3333-548a-b362-3f6432d42f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670429} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ac2d04-d1f7-52b3-82d6-496a5e6809d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cac8a4fd-3333-548a-b362-3f6432d42f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670429} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a0ab215-5ef6-5a4c-a729-1789edb119bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670446}] :question/id #uuid "615e1fed-a4a8-59b3-8748-a44e86a3ce62" :question/math-academy-id "q-130050" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Po}(5),$ compute $P(X=3),$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Po}(\\lambda ),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Po}(5),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{3}{e}^{-5}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.1404.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446748} {:answer/id #uuid "29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446750} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446786} {:answer/id #uuid "e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715e06f-e211-5b5b-b085-4dce3fe10cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278797} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae4dbb2-6216-51cc-9650-90dd9779ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260} {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fb5f2f-a80f-5006-a9c4-be4750a711f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280294} {:answer/id #uuid "e19f9127-76af-507a-8b89-9b102194e39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a1c281-1dcb-592f-8bb1-786e3c4e7bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" :question/math-academy-id "q-162790" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f063173-e6fe-5d2a-99b7-378065d2b1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308984} {:answer/id #uuid "40cef204-8013-5a17-b8d9-ae936e8062e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309242} {:answer/id #uuid "6700055d-6c65-5ea7-bc2b-4856d3527804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} {:answer/id #uuid "67a87526-29fc-5bf6-8e12-907f098b41ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309542} {:answer/id #uuid "8a51098b-f1b1-5736-8996-e4133727d23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6700055d-6c65-5ea7-bc2b-4856d3527804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b23d5d-5883-57bd-8005-c893ed0c9ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0163f286-fc30-51ed-907e-500011ffd762" :question/math-academy-id "q-337541" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89e8227a-a21e-51d1-92e0-947e1e75d9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197547} {:answer/id #uuid "9e490a88-3cfe-518e-a669-c4bca5084f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197548} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeef0c9-8497-5a8b-8705-e583cc0896c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197549} {:answer/id #uuid "f081f56f-4d9d-51a0-aa93-86bf9926b018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197550} {:answer/id #uuid "fae80050-937d-51f5-858f-521dcefb84ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e490a88-3cfe-518e-a669-c4bca5084f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197548} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f61eef3-fecf-50ec-985f-6e7b0490b34c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad6a612f-7b80-5294-8d9b-5ae88e52a2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-37781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-1\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(-2)^{2}=9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 3),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(0,-3)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $6$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,3),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a437b9ff-ebf6-5b44-bf7f-be36e82b3f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058843} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c792e2-a542-5e9b-a88b-cedeb1c0150e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058844} {:answer/id #uuid "b746e675-67cc-5f10-9cfb-c66b3279686b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058845} {:answer/id #uuid "cbccd66e-1635-58c5-97c9-56ffb4fd0b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058846} {:answer/id #uuid "021479db-7ec1-5c8a-bdb8-49cdc6d17874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058847}] :answer-field/id #uuid "78bcad99-fb77-58a5-a3c3-0330563127da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058842}] :question/id #uuid "eb4b681b-2ab2-5879-bf11-cb5badc0bb00" :question/math-academy-id "q-67308" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{x} = \\frac{1}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c71a3a11-3eba-5020-9597-3de35a5ab785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058867} {:answer/id #uuid "c768ab08-49e9-5e5c-928d-98edae762f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058868} {:answer/id #uuid "cf329fcc-fb6f-59d1-adcc-57bc40719b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058869} {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} {:answer/id #uuid "2c81b1e5-f63c-5724-81ac-8400764b9ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfde7113-aa52-5603-817f-a4c288821339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b4e7858-1264-5def-86e5-7598a8adf5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67329" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-2x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe93af-c815-5f2c-ac75-9a584d561ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d50060-ed5a-55ba-98a5-e136d622f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7edad-f24a-5a39-b3a9-bdf0e222d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058881} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} {:answer/id #uuid "4335e387-f8cb-50d1-a3a5-f1a739c9876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} :answer-field/id #uuid "084ec80c-c0ed-58a4-b621-614f03d00659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67332" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8be19f3-d1d1-5874-a267-0e4a8dae558d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058885} {:answer/id #uuid "df043132-a831-5ea4-b43a-b3fdb0795ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058886} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d6978c-429a-5678-9673-1dad8790f86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058887} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d08e45-da77-54d2-b0fb-f110b3c9d317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058888} {:answer/id #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058889} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4328795-e359-574d-9185-453cc4f416b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bbc3969-7e62-5072-9fb8-19f0fe79bb27" :question/math-academy-id "q-67355" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07077571-ccfa-55c8-b056-9eadc235f88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670390} {:answer/id #uuid "13a9c806-db29-54a1-9acb-eb652ed5bda7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670393} {:answer/id #uuid "67d51d27-80e7-57c7-9e05-09bbbcbfdd05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670410} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0f86d5-45cf-5956-9b36-1b1a2b6c7cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670411} {:answer/id #uuid "e665d509-094d-5efe-a8f8-5eec68cbb061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f0f86d5-45cf-5956-9b36-1b1a2b6c7cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670411} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eb74dc9-f9e4-508a-9d25-51c3d03237f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670442}] :question/id #uuid "b88ef01b-7c41-5a6e-8aa7-443a1edbe511" :question/math-academy-id "q-67405" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{2}{3x}=\\frac{2}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3x}{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{-2}\\right) & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\cancel{(-2)}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\right) & =\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{-9}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ 3x & =-9 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(-9) \\\\ \\cancel{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) & =-\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17967871-892e-54d8-8996-0c75074927db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670394} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd1339c-78ce-5ee3-8f74-f53c0bcbc2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670397} {:answer/id #uuid "40ba6c76-9a3f-5ef2-9896-7d65d8074f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670404} {:answer/id #uuid "a753380d-b5f3-5c93-aee1-5e8ad3488486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670423} {:answer/id #uuid "f42c1b0b-b25d-52c2-91a2-615fa34bd380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a753380d-b5f3-5c93-aee1-5e8ad3488486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670423} :answer-field/id #uuid "903ad0ec-920e-53b7-bf64-52c2cd5fad4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670449}] :question/id #uuid "c2a9d9f4-d0de-5d14-970c-e25e6928e45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67406" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} & =4\\cdot \\frac{x}{4} \\\\ \\frac{20}{2} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 10 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503171} {:answer/id #uuid "7733ee4f-eef5-5fd6-a757-f34e447b3828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503219} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7c6b7-28c9-5bea-bee5-7bb87f769d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503246} {:answer/id #uuid "c044e13e-665f-594c-8087-19c0c493d990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503366} {:answer/id #uuid "da440c13-f203-5583-aa96-40df058f74cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503171} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1354d1c-de34-5200-8c22-d682a6a448d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" :question/math-academy-id "q-67407" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 5 & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a892c61-4f79-54af-b709-acd39c2aec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670396} {:answer/id #uuid "642a1d35-9969-5aab-aa7b-17ece55cc2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670409} {:answer/id #uuid "cacfc459-0129-548d-b30c-8572a68d8d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670430} {:answer/id #uuid "d2014d78-0172-5bf0-a874-4c36d842905a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670431} {:answer/id #uuid "f71b448e-c54f-5b5b-8deb-0cb850d4f2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71b448e-c54f-5b5b-8deb-0cb850d4f2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670440} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3d2617-e9dc-5524-9ebf-d810c3ed7f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670445}] :question/id #uuid "9691bd1b-5514-58d9-8dcd-30ee08809ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67410" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{5}{3x}=\\frac{5}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3x}{5} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ (-5)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{-5}\\right) & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ (\\cancel{-5})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{\\cancel{-5}}\\right) & =(\\cancel{5})\\cdot \\frac{-6}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ 3x & =-6 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) & =-\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d3d307-80c5-5100-8287-638a2bdecc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502991} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1f877-23e8-59f2-a2aa-0744c5e7dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503013} {:answer/id #uuid "33f90d44-4628-5b5e-b9c2-d33e90471828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503075} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef03d2c-7e33-54bd-ac16-3e50ecf310c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503166} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503463} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ae6554-557c-5337-b681-7691c583fdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67416" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{2}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019bbc5c-24ab-5bb5-b14d-a699d32aaf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670387} {:answer/id #uuid "36ada7f6-3acb-5ed8-854e-16dfc1570278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670402} {:answer/id #uuid "61f2c8da-5821-5a73-a9dd-a060e8a03802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670408} {:answer/id #uuid "b2901323-534c-5ab7-b861-47e3c05e86d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670424} {:answer/id #uuid "ba977e39-5aa4-530f-bd63-3166457cb280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ada7f6-3acb-5ed8-854e-16dfc1570278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670402} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac62122-9a87-592f-8c40-d2cc5cd0d361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670447}] :question/id #uuid "4d4080c2-4f5b-58b8-ad8e-0c4cd273e19f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67421" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{x-7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=7$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{6}{1}=\\frac{x-7}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-6=x-7\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =x-7 \\\\ -6+7 & =x-7+7 \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e09809f-1ac1-5163-95fe-56a6c3cca096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503015} {:answer/id #uuid "1702597c-5660-544c-a89c-cba9f00560d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503028} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503069} {:answer/id #uuid "78dcdf47-9629-5ab8-a15d-6bafe37b89e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503225} {:answer/id #uuid "94f3bb0d-e212-55c1-a64b-282f04cc5137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503069} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" :question/math-academy-id "q-67422" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0846c466-61cd-5359-8ea4-e12984fd5153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670391} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb090e7-b1fa-569e-a88c-88009df2442e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670395} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee2b8c6-b052-5be6-aad0-5d83cf554aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670399} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2a2b8c-7fc2-5164-bd7c-8939dda9fe34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670415} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6e5e61-3cdc-568d-bbcf-d767dfaf6770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0846c466-61cd-5359-8ea4-e12984fd5153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670391} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2033eef-36dd-539d-9289-20b6ea9c55f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670450}] :question/id #uuid "af124b69-d0c1-5851-8687-aa12571f64c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-67424" :question/problem "Solve the equation  $\\frac{1}{2x+1}=\\frac{3}{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{1}=\\frac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $3.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+3}{3}=\\frac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{6x+3}{3} & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 6x+3 & =5x \\\\ 6x+3-3 & =5x-3 \\\\ 6x & =5x-3 \\\\ 6x-5x & =5x-3-5x \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a6fcca-aad9-5ad1-a121-fdaf665d5aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503145} {:answer/id #uuid "d92f70d4-3067-5353-97b7-e64c25a54de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503415} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503427} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db71b8-ad73-5cce-b817-b4e5c4f03d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503441} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb895ed-3b69-53d2-9ba7-f858762d5ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503427} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67425" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{9}{3x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\cdot 6x & =\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a3543e-a251-579f-b8fc-7f9ba24706ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571566} {:answer/id #uuid "1621b1c6-95e0-5b2c-bd45-64e852812aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571579} {:answer/id #uuid "65daad1e-0611-5bd7-afbf-4fd9443314b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571712} {:answer/id #uuid "adca5567-c435-5016-bb46-aa649ccb320e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571836} {:answer/id #uuid "fb02bb4e-edfe-536a-8159-eeaa09da9182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a3543e-a251-579f-b8fc-7f9ba24706ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571566} :answer-field/id #uuid "b35c0d69-6d83-53fa-b5c9-4ab6a701cd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50efbd3d-db78-53ec-922c-e4529ac97e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-67426" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-5}=\\frac{2}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{6}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{6}=\\frac{3x}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x}{6} \\\\ x-5 & =3x \\\\ x-x-5 & =3x-x \\\\ -5 & =2x \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (-5) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (2x) \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fb6247-b859-5d61-b518-790cfca6cb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670388} {:answer/id #uuid "2f58ab42-b2b4-57a7-879c-22999c2cbd3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670400} {:answer/id #uuid "305d2d20-219b-50fa-9e4a-abb139156c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670401} {:answer/id #uuid "9179637f-1c4b-575f-87a9-f66b0e1ed283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670418} {:answer/id #uuid "efd6b795-0b9d-5161-940a-359aa2c330a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "305d2d20-219b-50fa-9e4a-abb139156c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670401} :answer-field/id #uuid "c08dc095-4a06-5102-a53c-3507e8db203b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670451}] :question/id #uuid "e5e7057b-4215-507e-8d16-5466bad05ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-67441" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2x+1}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{5}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $5.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{5}=\\frac{5x+5}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5)\\cdot \\frac{2x+1}{5} & =(5)\\cdot \\frac{5x+5}{5} \\\\ 2x+1 & =5x+5 \\\\ 1-5 & =5x-2x \\\\ -4 & =3x \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-4) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-4) & =(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0357732-7d25-5b9d-85e1-5cab3acc7ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2691" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165867} {:answer/id #uuid "b8dae3a7-91fb-52c0-b0bc-cdbf28927294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2249" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165868} {:answer/id #uuid "0aeeb7c9-2abe-518f-b9b1-a59adb1fe821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165869} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2be036-5f5e-5179-889b-f45060731336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165870} {:answer/id #uuid "715aef0b-7e80-505a-90f3-c0bb91180806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1804" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165871}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbb5c90-610e-5a65-8619-e17d9a570b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165866}] :question/id #uuid "4d4292bf-c73b-5439-8dac-84cef869be2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95993" :question/problem "Given $X∼Po(2)$, compute $P(X = 3)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4266e4f5-01a4-59c0-9d1a-6a59858398c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670405} {:answer/id #uuid "78b3c30e-bbe9-50c9-a378-76cb62a0a57a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0361" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670414} {:answer/id #uuid "8d665c00-37aa-5b15-bca3-222341cd0fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1014" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670417} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa4d3d-3196-543c-b2e9-3de9e87a5486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0802" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670421} {:answer/id #uuid "d62cc53f-5c2d-54bf-8c03-97c83dc95c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78b3c30e-bbe9-50c9-a378-76cb62a0a57a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0361" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670414} :answer-field/id #uuid "11838f10-dc22-5f3b-ba56-0d91440185de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670443}] :question/id #uuid "967d2e95-57ac-5662-bd22-c0decd75429c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96847" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Po}(2),$ compute $P(X=5),$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Po}(\\lambda ),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Po}(2),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{2}^{x}{e}^{-2}}{x!}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}{e}^{-2}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.0361.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c1fa27-09bb-576b-97dd-85dd401dc8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360976} {:answer/id #uuid "681a4a87-3b1d-58fd-b752-c86f6763e6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361305} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e663-64a3-56ec-a432-8a3dc5cde46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361480} {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38c98d-8f6b-5cf8-b355-869da372bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" :question/math-academy-id "q-99240" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$"}]}
